WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.460
Welcome to The Explainer. Have you ever been handed this incredibly ambitious goal, a super tight deadline, and absolutely zero direct reports? Yeah, if you're a product manager, that's literally just a Tuesday. Today, we're diving into the senior PM playbook for leading without authority. We're going to figure out exactly how top-tier leaders drive massive results relying entirely on influence.

2
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.620
So here's the core dilemma, right?

3
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.540
How do you lead when absolutely no one reports to you?

4
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:31.140
In a normal corporate hierarchy, your rank dictates action.

5
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.860
But the second you step into a PM role, that hierarchy just vanishes.

6
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.220
The developers, they don't report to you.

7
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.380
The designers definitely don't owe you anything.

8
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.460
In the data team, well, they're already swamped with someone else's project.

9
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.420
Yet somehow, your entire success is measured by your ability to get all of these brilliant,

10
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.360
fiercely autonomous people moving in the exact same direction.

11
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:59.200
Because at the end of the day, brilliant strategy and beautiful roadmaps mean absolutely nothing if people don't actively choose to follow you.

12
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.760
To really ground these concepts, let's meet Mikul.

13
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.660
She's going to be our narrative guide for this explainer.

14
00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.620
Mikul is a senior product manager at a fast-paced sauce startup called Velux.

15
00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:21.180
And every single day, she is facing these high-stakes scenarios where she has to guide developers, designers, and even C-suite executives, all without the power to issue a single direct order.

16
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:22.640
No way, right?

17
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.220
Well, let's see what we can actually learn from how she pulls this off.

18
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.080
Okay, let's dive right into this roadmap.

19
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.960
We've got five essential areas to master today.

20
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.440
First, leading without direct authority.

21
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.460
Second, tailoring your message.

22
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.520
Third, mapping key stakeholders.

23
00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.120
Fourth, navigating collaborative conflict.

24
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.560
And finally, negotiating interests.

25
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:44.660
Let's get into it.

26
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.520
Section 1, reading without direct authority.

27
00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:55.520
You know, the first thing I found really fascinating about Michelle's playbook is her strict rule against relying on borrowed authority.

28
00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.660
We've all been tempted to say, well, the CEO asked for this, right?

29
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:04.700
And sure, that might win you a quick argument today, but it completely erodes the team's respect over time.

30
00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.740
Instead, she relies on four core pullers.

31
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:11.900
First is influence, which means combining hard data with genuine empathy.

32
00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.400
Second is trust, which is just built slowly, day by day, through kept promises.

33
00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:21.420
Third, vision, because let's face it, people mobilize for meaning, not just a list of tasks.

34
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:27.080
And fourth is execution, which means getting your hands dirty and actively removing blockers for your team.

35
00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.100
Actually, a really great industry pro tip here.

36
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:39.040
If you combine these four pillars with Kim Scott's concept of radical candor, you know, caring personally but challenging directly, it builds an immense amount of trust.

37
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:45.640
To see what this kind of influence looks like in action, just look at how Mischel handled a massive tech debt crisis.

38
00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:49.760
She urgently needed the dev team to dedicate a full sprint to tech debt.

39
00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.940
But of course, the executives only wanted flashy new features.

40
00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:53.900
So what did she do?

41
00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:55.400
She didn't issue commands.

42
00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.700
Instead, she sat down with the lead developer,

43
00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.900
looked at the messy code with real empathy,

44
00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:03.180
and brought just one extremely sharp data point to the table.

45
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:04.520
31%.

46
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.780
She showed that 31% of all customer support tickets

47
00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.280
were stemming directly from synchronization failures.

48
00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.840
Then she turned right around and showed the executives

49
00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.840
how fixing this one issue would actually accelerate their next two flashy features.

50
00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:20.280
And just like that, the sprint was approved, not a single order given.

51
00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.020
Moving on to section two, tailoring your message.

52
00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:29.060
You can be literally the sharpest product manager in the room, but here's the truth.

53
00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.460
If your message isn't perfectly calibrated for the specific audience you're talking to,

54
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:34.640
you've already failed.

55
00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.700
As a PM, you have to be a master translator.

56
00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:41.320
Shifting your focus based on who is in the room is absolutely key.

57
00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:42.520
I mean, think about it.

58
00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:46.460
You wouldn't hand a developer a high-level executive ROI report, right?

59
00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.860
Nor would you give the CEO a granular list of technical trade-offs.

60
00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.400
Customers, they just want value and simplicity.

61
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:54.760
Meanwhile, your core team, they need vision.

62
00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:55.820
They need inspiration.

63
00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.040
Oh, and remember, the format totally matters too.

64
00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.040
A quick three-sentence Slack message is great for coordination,

65
00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.000
but please don't use it to replace a six-page strategic narrative document.

66
00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.560
Now, a brilliantly effective way to execute this,

67
00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.480
especially for executives, is the bluff method.

68
00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:18.940
That stands for bottom line up front. Take a look at this comparison. On one side, we have a typical

69
00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:23.860
rambling technical email that Mikai initially drafted for her CEO about delaying a launch.

70
00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:29.300
It totally buries the lead under a mountain of context. But on the other side, that's the bluff

71
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:34.900
version she actually sent. The very first line hits you right away. Recommendation, delay launch by

72
00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:41.300
14 days. Bam. Then it's followed by crisp, clear bullet points detailing the security vulnerability,

73
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.860
the cost of delaying, and the massive business risk of not delaying.

74
00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:46.640
The result of doing it this way?

75
00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:50.200
Her CEO approved it from his phone in exactly 20 minutes.

76
00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.340
So yeah, always put the bottom line up front.

77
00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.280
All right, section three, mapping your key stakeholders.

78
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.740
As a PM, you're sitting at this completely chaotic intersection.

79
00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.020
The CEO has a grand vision, sales is sweating over a quota,

80
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:09.040
and literally everyone thinks the product is built just for their specific department.

81
00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:09.980
It's a lot.

82
00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:12.560
To survive this, you need a solid system.

83
00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:16.560
Step one, identify a stakeholder's actual power to influence the product.

84
00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.440
Step two, gauge their true interest level in the day-to-day weeds of what you're doing.

85
00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.200
And step three, refresh this map constantly.

86
00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:24.380
Why do we do this?

87
00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.740
Because the golden rule of stakeholder management is zero surprises.

88
00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.580
Seriously, a stakeholder who hears bad news early, that's a partner.

89
00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:35.300
But a stakeholder who hears that same bad news for the first time in a crowded meeting,

90
00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.060
that is an adversary for sure.

91
00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:42.180
So in practice, this just means tailoring your engagement based on where people fall on the map.

92
00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:47.660
Your high-power, high-interest folks, they need to be managed closely, usually with regular one-on-ones.

93
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.220
But if they have high power and low interest, like, say, a CFO, you just need to keep them satisfied with high-level updates.

94
00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.600
Do not drown them in the technical details.

95
00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.360
Micho uses this exact mapping exercise right before every single quarterly review.

96
00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:05.700
By pre-aligning with the CEO and the VP of sales based on their specific quadrants,

97
00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:10.600
By the time she gets to the actual roadmap meeting, it's completely smooth, totally argument-free.

98
00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:15.860
Which brings us to Section 4, Navigating Collaborative Conflict.

99
00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:20.860
Look, when passionate people work together, conflict is a feature, not a bug.

100
00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:21.740
It's going to happen.

101
00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:26.960
The trick is eliminating the messy, unnecessary confusion before that conflict even starts.

102
00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.340
And the RACI matrix is perfect for this.

103
00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:33.320
It clearly defines who is responsible, as in doing the actual work.

104
00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.220
Who is accountable, for final sign-off.

105
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:38.980
who needs to be consulted for input, and who just gets informed with updates.

106
00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:42.900
It literally stops those, wait, I thought you were handling that arguments cold.

107
00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.980
And I absolutely love this golden rule.

108
00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.240
If there is more than one person listed as accountable, then absolutely no one is accountable.

109
00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:52.640
Okay, but what happens when you actually do clash?

110
00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:57.120
Well, the Thomas-Kilman model identifies five distinct conflict styles,

111
00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:00.140
and it maps them based on assertiveness versus cooperativeness.

112
00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:02.760
You can compete and just force your way through.

113
00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.600
You can compromise and awkwardly split the difference.

114
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.260
You can avoid the issue entirely, or you can just accommodate the other person and give in.

115
00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:15.340
But if you're a product manager, collaborating is your absolute superpower.

116
00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:20.660
It means being highly assertive about the business goals, but highly cooperative with the people involved.

117
00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:26.960
It is definitely not about settling for some weak half-measure compromise where everybody leaves the room slightly unhappy.

118
00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.140
It's about digging deep to find a genuinely mutually beneficial solution.

119
00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:34.460
Because remember, since you don't actually manage anyone,

120
00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:39.280
relying on true collaboration is what builds that long-term trust you need to survive in this role.

121
00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.600
And when you inevitably have to have a tough conversation,

122
00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:45.260
especially when you're managing up to your boss,

123
00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:48.600
the SBI feedback model is an absolute lifesaver.

124
00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.600
It stands for Situation, Behavior, Impact.

125
00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:55.620
So instead of making an emotional judgment like you don't respect the design team,

126
00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:56.880
you frame it objectively.

127
00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:00.260
You say, hey, yesterday in the meeting, that's the situation,

128
00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.360
When you interrupted the designer, there's the observable behavior.

129
00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:06.140
It caused the rest of the team to stop sharing their ideas.

130
00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:07.680
And that is the impact.

131
00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:08.580
See how that works?

132
00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:13.920
It completely strips the heated emotion away and focuses everyone purely on solving the problem together.

133
00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:19.840
Which brings us to our final section, section 5, negotiating interests, not positions.

134
00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:26.260
Since you lack formal authority, principled negotiation is basically your sharpest tool for getting alignment.

135
00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.600
Think about the classic story of two sisters fighting over an orange.

136
00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.420
Their position, what they're loudly demanding, is I want the orange.

137
00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.020
Now, a simple compromise just cuts it right down the middle, leaving them both with less than they wanted.

138
00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:42.500
But if you take a second to investigate their interests, like why do they actually want it,

139
00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:47.400
you might discover that one sister just wants the juice to drink, and the other just needs the peel to bake a cake.

140
00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:50.880
By focusing on those underlying interests rather than their rigid positions,

141
00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:54.340
both sisters can walk away with 100% of what they actually need.

142
00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:55.720
It's a real game-changer.

143
00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:00.620
But before you can start finding those interests, you absolutely must know your BATNA.

144
00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:03.160
That's your best alternative to a negotiated agreement.

145
00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:05.260
Basically, it's your walkaway plan.

146
00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:10.260
Going into any negotiation without a solid alternative makes every single no feel like a fatal threat.

147
00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:14.300
Knowing your BATNA beforehand, that keeps you calm, it gives you crucial leverage,

148
00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.760
and it really helps you define the zone of possible agreement.

149
00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.780
Let's see how Mischel applies this framework in real life.

150
00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:23.440
Imagine a massive enterprise client threatens VLOX saying,

151
00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:27.280
Build us a custom dashboard right now or we are canceling our contract.

152
00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:29.900
Their position is clearly a custom dashboard,

153
00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.180
and building that would eat up an entire incredibly expensive development sprint.

154
00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:35.800
But Mishel just pauses.

155
00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.280
She checks her BATNA.

156
00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:38.160
Losing this client?

157
00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:40.800
Yeah, it would sting, but it wouldn't bankrupt the company.

158
00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:42.400
So she's got room to breathe.

159
00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.900
Instead of fighting with them over the roadmap, she just asks why they need it.

160
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.840
Turns out, the client's true interest is just,

161
00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:52.580
I need a quarterly data report to show my boss.

162
00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:53.580
Boom.

163
00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:58.660
Mischel completely bypasses that costly development sprint just by giving them a quick, automated

164
00:09:58.660 --> 00:09:59.280
report template.

165
00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:01.580
They found the juice, and they found the peel.

166
00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:04.640
And that brings us to our core takeaway for this explainer.

167
00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:08.880
Zero surprises and high trust are the real currency of product management.

168
00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:13.660
Leading without authority, it isn't some magical, innate personality trait that you're

169
00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:14.320
just born with.

170
00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:15.060
Not at all.

171
00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:16.980
It's a systematic process.

172
00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:21.820
It's about gathering hard data, tailoring your communication, actively mapping out your

173
00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:27.340
stakeholders, collaborating through the inevitable conflicts, and constantly, constantly negotiating

174
00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:32.080
for those underlying interests. I'm going to leave you with this one thought to take into your work

175
00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:36.580
week. In your very next cross-functional meeting, are you going to be stubbornly defending your

176
00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:41.840
position, or will you take a step back to uncover their true interests? Your answer to that is what

177
00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:46.920
dictates whether you are simply managing a bunch of tasks or truly leading a product. Thanks so

178
00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.800
much for joining me on this explainer, and I'll see you next time.

